    <body>
      <sec id="article-56125.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Percutaneously inserted ventricular assist devices (pVADs) are increasingly used in the management of acute and chronic heart failure, particularly in cardiogenic shock and high-risk percutaneous coronary intervention. These nonpulsatile axial flow pumps support circulation by unloading the ventricles and improving cardiac output. Advances in device technology have expanded available options, allowing tailored levels of support based on clinical need. This activity reviews left- and right-sided pVADs, including design, mechanisms, indications, contraindications, insertion techniques, and complications. It also highlights key clinical data and comparisons with other mechanical circulatory support strategies, with emphasis on patient selection and device choice. Participants will strengthen their ability to apply pVAD therapy effectively while reinforcing interprofessional collaboration to optimize patient outcomes and safety.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify the clinical indications, contraindications, and patient populations appropriate for percutaneously inserted ventricular assist devices.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Differentiate between low-, intermediate-, and high-flow percutaneous ventricular assist devices based on hemodynamic support needs and procedural considerations.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Evaluate the potential complications of percutaneous ventricular assist devices and appropriate strategies for mitigation and management.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with the interprofessional healthcare team to optimize the selection, placement, and ongoing management of mechanical circulatory support in patients with cardiogenic shock or undergoing high-risk interventions.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=56125&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=56125">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-56125.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Percutaneous mechanical circulatory support devices have become essential tools in the management of cardiogenic shock and high-risk cardiac interventions. The evolution of these devices began with the introduction of the intra-aortic balloon pump (IABP) in the 1960s, which gained popularity due to its ease of use and percutaneous access.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-56125.r1">[1]</xref> However, the IABP offers only modest hemodynamic benefit, typically increasing cardiac output by approximately 0.5 L/min, which limits its utility in patients with severe hemodynamic compromise.</p>
        <p>In response to this need, percutaneous ventricular assist devices (pVADs) that utilize axial-flow technology were developed to provide more substantial hemodynamic support. The percutaneous left ventricular assist device (pLVAD) is a catheter-mounted, nonpulsatile axial flow pump that is inserted retrograde across the aortic valve and pumps blood from the left ventricle (LV) into the ascending aorta.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-56125.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;Since its initial United States Food and Drug Administration (FDA) approval in 2008, multiple generations and flow capacities have emerged. These include the original 2.5 L/min version, an intermediate model offering 3.0 to 4.0 L/min, and a surgically implanted high-flow version capable of delivering up to 5.5 L/min.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-56125.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-56125.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-56125.r5">[5]</xref> These devices can improve cardiac output, reduce myocardial oxygen demand, lower left ventricular end-diastolic pressure (LVEDP), and enhance systemic perfusion and coronary blood flow.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-56125.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <p>Hemodynamic unloading of the&#x000a0;LV also reduces pulmonary congestion and left atrial pressures, indirectly improving right ventricular (RV) afterload and overall biventricular performance. In parallel, percutaneous right ventricular assist devices (pRVADs) have been developed to support patients with isolated or predominant&#x000a0;RV failure. These devices are typically inserted via femoral venous access, with inflow from the inferior vena cava and outflow into the pulmonary artery, and can provide up to 4.0 L/min of support.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-56125.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>As the adoption of&#x000a0;percutaneous mechanical circulatory support expands, accumulating data continues to refine its appropriate use. Although these devices offer clear physiologic benefits, their impact on clinical outcomes remains an active area of investigation. Clinicians must integrate patient selection, device capabilities, and timing of initiation into a comprehensive, evidence-informed approach to care.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-56125.s3" sec-type="Anatomy and Physiology">
        <title>Anatomy and Physiology</title>
        <p>Through single femoral arterial access, the pLVAD crosses the aortic valve in a retrograde fashion, with the inflow positioned in the&#x000a0;LV and the outflow in the ascending aorta.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-56125.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>In contrast, the pRVAD requires femoral venous access. The device is advanced and positioned across the tricuspid and pulmonary valves, allowing it to draw blood from the inferior vena cava and eject it into the pulmonary artery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-56125.r8">[8]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-56125.s4" sec-type="Indications">
        <title>Indications</title>
        <p>The following are indications for pVADs:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>High-risk percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI)</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>There is no universally accepted definition of high-risk PCI; however, clinical and anatomic factors such as impaired&#x000a0;LV function, left main disease, ostial lesions, severe calcification, and cardiogenic shock are key considerations. pLVADs, such as the Impella&#x000ae; system, can provide&#x000a0;hemodynamic support and reduce periprocedural complications. Clinical studies suggest that their use may improve longer-term outcomes when employed early and in accordance with best practices. The decision to use mechanical support should be individualized based on heart team assessment and patient-specific risk factors.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-56125.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-56125.r10">[10]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Acute myocardial infarction</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Some patients with ST-elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI) or non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction (NSTEMI) may benefit from circulatory support to unload the LV and improve coronary perfusion.&#x000a0;Recent randomized data suggest that early mechanical circulatory support during acute myocardial infarction complicated by cardiogenic shock may improve survival and potentially reduce myocardial injury. However, further studies are warranted to confirm mechanisms and refine patient selection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-56125.r11">[11]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Cardiogenic shock and advanced heart failure</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Mechanical circulatory support can stabilize critically ill patients and serve as a bridge to recovery, surgical intervention, durable mechanical support, or cardiac transplantation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-56125.r4">[4]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Percutaneous aortic interventions</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Selected high-risk patients undergoing balloon aortic valvuloplasty or transcatheter aortic valve replacement may benefit from temporary mechanical support.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-56125.r12">[12]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Electrophysiologic procedures</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Patients with severe LV dysfunction undergoing electrophysiologic procedures may require support, as they may not tolerate prolonged ventricular arrhythmias during the intervention.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-56125.r13">[13]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>RV failure</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>The pRVAD is indicated in cases of acute RV failure. The RECOVER RIGHT trial demonstrated both the safety and the immediate hemodynamic benefits of this device in such patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-56125.r7">[7]</xref> In addition to cardiogenic shock from traditional causes, adjunct percutaneous right-sided support may be considered in carefully selected cases of massive pulmonary embolism, particularly when patients exhibit refractory shock or are not candidates for systemic thrombolysis. In this context, mechanical support may serve as a bridge to recovery or definitive intervention by unloading the failing RV and improving end-organ perfusion.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-56125.r14">[14]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-56125.s5" sec-type="Contraindications">
        <title>Contraindications</title>
        <p>Contraindications to pLVADs include:&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Significant peripheral vascular disease</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Aortic stenosis (valve area &#x0003c;1.5 cm&#x000b2;) or aortic insufficiency</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Ventricular septal defect
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>The pLVAD can significantly increase left-to-right shunting.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>LV thrombus</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>In the presence of&#x000a0;1 or more of these contraindications, an alternative modality of mechanical circulatory support should be considered.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-56125.r3">[3]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-56125.s6" sec-type="Equipment">
        <title>Equipment</title>
        <p>The pLVAD is a 12 Fr pump mounted on a 9 Fr catheter. A console is used to control the device's performance and display the pressure gradient between the inflow and outflow. A purge solution is delivered through a sidearm at the distal end of the 9 Fr catheter to prevent blood from entering the motor. Additionally, pressure-monitoring tubing is connected to enable real-time hemodynamic assessment.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-56125.s7" sec-type="Personnel">
        <title>Personnel</title>
        <p>Interventional cardiologists typically place low-flow and intermediate-flow assist devices percutaneously under fluoroscopic guidance. High-flow assist devices, which require surgical graft and cutdown for insertion, are generally positioned by cardiothoracic surgeons or via a heart team approach in tandem with interventional cardiology.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-56125.s8" sec-type="Preparation">
        <title>Preparation</title>
        <p>Before device insertion, vascular access should be evaluated using imaging modalities such as ultrasound or angiography to assess vessel size, tortuosity, and the presence of peripheral arterial disease. Anticoagulation is initiated to reduce the risk of thrombotic complications, and baseline hemodynamic parameters should be obtained. The interprofessional team should confirm device sizing, verify the availability of necessary equipment, and address patient-specific considerations, such as the presence of mechanical valves or thrombus burden.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-56125.s9" sec-type="Technique or Treatment">
        <title>Technique or Treatment</title>
        <p>The low- and intermediate-flow pLVAD is a 12 Fr pump mounted on a 9 Fr catheter shaft, advanced via femoral artery access and positioned across the aortic valve into the LV under fluoroscopic guidance. The high-flow pLVAD (5.5 L/min) also mounts on the same 9 Fr catheter shaft, but the pump itself is 22 Fr in diameter. Due to its larger size, the high-flow pLVAD requires a surgical graft with cutdown via the axillary or femoral artery for insertion.</p>
        <p>The 9 Fr catheter includes both purge and pressure lumens.&#x000a0;The proximal end of the catheter features a hub for attaching the console cable, along with sidearms for infusion of purge solution and pressure-monitoring tubing.</p>
        <p>The console enables management of pump speed across&#x000a0;9 performance levels and displays the pressure gradient between the inflow and outflow ports.</p>
        <p>Intravenous heparin is recommended during device deployment to achieve an activated clotting time (ACT) of 250 to 500 seconds. After placement, ACT should be maintained between 160 and 180 seconds to prevent thrombus formation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-56125.r15">[15]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-56125.s10" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Potential complications of pVADs&#x000a0;include hemolysis due to mechanical erythrocyte shearing and acute kidney injury resulting from persistent hemolysis. Limb ischemia may occur, particularly in patients with peripheral arterial disease or those who have undergone prolonged large-bore arterial access. Vascular complications can include hematoma, pseudoaneurysm, arteriovenous fistula, and retroperitoneal hemorrhage. Bleeding may also necessitate a blood transfusion. In rare cases, pericardial tamponade may develop as a result of cardiac perforation or wire manipulation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-56125.r16">[16]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-56125.s11" sec-type="Clinical Significance">
        <title>Clinical Significance</title>
        <p>In clinical trials, pVADs (2.5 L/min) have been compared with IABPs. In the ISAR-SHOCK trial, 25 patients with acute myocardial infarction and severely reduced LV function were randomly assigned to either the pLVAD or IABP group. After 30 minutes of support, the cardiac index was significantly higher in the pLVAD group than in the IABP group; however, there was no difference in 30-day mortality between the&#x000a0;2 groups.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-56125.r17">[17]</xref></p>
        <p>The DanGer Shock trial evaluated early mechanical circulatory support with the percutaneously inserted Impella CP&#x000ae; device in patients with acute myocardial infarction complicated by cardiogenic shock. In this randomized controlled trial, 360 patients were assigned to either early Impella CP&#x000ae; support or standard medical therapy. At 30 days, the all-cause mortality rate was significantly lower in the Impella&#x000ae; group compared to the control group (45.8% vs. 58.5%). This trial provides the first randomized evidence that early&#x000a0;LV unloading may improve survival in this critically ill population.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-56125.r11">[11]</xref></p>
        <p>In the PROTECT II trial, 448 high-risk PCI patients were randomized to receive either an IABP or the percutaneously inserted Impella&#x000ae; device (2.5 L/min). Although the study ended early and showed no significant difference in 30-day major adverse events, per-protocol analysis revealed improved 90-day outcomes in the Impella&#x000ae; group, primarily due to fewer repeat revascularizations. The subsequent PROTECT III registry demonstrated that early use of the Impella&#x000ae; and adherence to best practices were associated with improved procedural safety and outcomes in real-world high-risk PCI.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-56125.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-56125.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>The RECOVER RIGHT trial prospectively evaluated 30 patients with refractory RV failure, 12 following myocardial infarction, and 18 postcardiac surgery. The study demonstrated that the pRVAD provided immediate hemodynamic improvement, including reductions in central venous pressure and increases in cardiac index. The 30-day survival rate in this cohort was 73.3%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-56125.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-56125.r18">[18]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-56125.s12" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Effective management of patients requiring a percutaneously inserted ventricular assist device (pVAD) demands advanced clinical skills, strategic decision-making, and seamless collaboration across an interprofessional team. Physicians and advanced practitioners must rapidly identify patients in cardiogenic shock who may benefit from mechanical circulatory support, interpret hemodynamic data, and determine candidacy for temporary or destination mechanical circulatory support. Procedural expertise, sterile technique, and mastery of vascular access and device positioning are essential for safe insertion. Nurses provide continuous bedside monitoring, manage device alarms, track perfusion markers, and detect early complications such as hemolysis, bleeding, limb ischemia, or infection. Pharmacists optimize anticoagulation and vasoactive therapies, prevent drug-device interactions, and ensure safe medication titration. At the same time, respiratory therapists, perfusionists, and rehabilitation specialists support organ function and recovery throughout the patient&#x02019;s course of care. Social workers and palliative care specialists further contribute by addressing psychosocial needs, goals of care, and complex decision-making.</p>
        <p>Initiating mechanical circulatory support, whether temporary or as destination therapy, requires coordinated interprofessional involvement and consistent communication grounded in mutual respect, shared goals, and ethical responsibility. Early identification is critical, and the formation of a dedicated cardiogenic shock team enables rapid diagnosis, structured protocols, reliable risk stratification, and timely initiation of the most appropriate device.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-56125.r19">[19]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-56125.r20">[20]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-56125.r21">[21]</xref> Daily multidisciplinary rounds, standardized handoffs, and clear delineation of responsibilities ensure that clinical findings are shared promptly, treatment plans are continuously refined, and patient-centered goals remain at the forefront. This collaborative, team-based approach enhances patient safety, reduces complications, and improves outcomes by ensuring streamlined communication, coordinated care, and cohesive management across all stages, from device implantation to stabilization, weaning, or transition to long-term support.</p>
      </sec>
